@TGT
Feature: a target whose fitness checks are converted scenarios

  Planted for @SCN-STG-005's @integration arm and used TWICE — this tree is byte-identical in
  the scn-tags-converted-unconfigured fixture beside it, and only the package.json differs.

  Both scenarios below carry the fitness axis tag and their @SCN and nothing else, which is
  the whole of what a converted check's scenario says about itself: the tag names the axis and
  the CHARACTERISTIC is on the configured row. Neither names a tier, so both red the floor
  unless the axis tag satisfies it.

  THE PAIR IS THE RULING RATHER THAN AN ACCIDENT OF THE PLANT. SCN-TGT-001 is named by a
  configured row and SCN-TGT-002 is named by none — a half-converted check, whose tag moved and
  whose row was never written. Both satisfy this floor. That second one IS a corpus fault, and
  it is @SCN-LNT-020's: it names the scenario by id, and the run reds on it regardless through
  the degenerate both-missing item. A floor redding it here too would put half of one fault in
  a second place with the wrong remedy attached — name a tier, said to an author whose scenario
  names its axis.

  AND THE SISTER TREE CONFIGURES NO ROWS AT ALL, where both scenarios still pass. Which words
  name a CHECK is the target's answer; which axis a scenario is ON is the model's, exactly as
  `unit` is — so the axis word is not the target's to grant or to withhold, and a floor gating
  it on the manifest would disagree with the obligation rule, which reads the word with no such
  gate, on precisely this corpus.

  @fitness @SCN-TGT-001
  Scenario: the codebase stays inside the complexity thresholds

  @fitness @SCN-TGT-002
  Scenario: a converted scenario no configured row names
